C++ Combinator DSL build and installation requirements
======================================================

The complete build consists of a native C++ library, examples, CREPL,
benchmarks and tests, plus a separate Emscripten build of Combinator Studio.
The complete native CREPL dependency build is POSIX-oriented.  Use Linux,
macOS, or WSL on Windows.

Required tools
--------------

The tools needed depend on which CMake options are enabled:

* CMake 3.20 or newer is required for every CMake build.  This is stronger
  than a 3.2 minimum because the current build uses newer CMake facilities,
  including string(JSON), FindPython3 and REQUIRED find_program calls.
* A C++ compiler and standard library with C++20 support are required for all
  C++ targets.
* A C compiler with C11 support is required.  The verified C compiler is
  passed to the downloaded ncurses and readline builds used by CREPL.
* Native threading support is required for native builds.
* GNU Make, installed as either make or gmake, is required when
  COMBDSL_BUILD_EXAMPLES is enabled.  BSD make is not sufficient for the
  ncurses and readline dependency builds.
* Git is required when COMBDSL_BUILD_EXAMPLES is enabled so CMake can obtain
  the pinned readline source revision.
* A normal POSIX userland is required by the ncurses and readline configure,
  build and installation scripts.  CMake explicitly checks for sed, awk and
  install; those scripts exercise the shell and also use conventional tools
  such as grep, expr, mkdir, rm, cp and ln.
* Python 3.7 or newer is required on UNIX when CREPL and the test suite are
  enabled.  It runs three interactive CREPL tests covering completion,
  persistence, and terminal behavior and color.  Those tests use forkpty,
  signals and termios and do not run under native Windows.
* Node.js 18 or newer and npm are required when COMBDSL_BUILD_TESTS is
  enabled.  Node.js runs the Studio tests through node --test.  npm is also
  required by the separate Sites packaging and publishing workflow.
* An activated Emscripten SDK providing emcmake, emcc and em++ is required
  only when COMBDSL_BUILD_BROWSER is enabled.

CMake verifies the listed compiler-language support and executable tools
where applicable.  The remaining filesystem, POSIX-userland and network
requirements are exercised during the build.  Checks are scoped to their
consumers: a project which only includes combDSL as a header-only subproject
is not required to install CREPL, test, browser or publishing tools.

Network access and downloaded dependencies
------------------------------------------

A top-level configuration requires TLS-enabled network access to query the
latest fmt release metadata from GitHub.  A complete build additionally
obtains:

* the verified archive for that latest fmt release from GitHub;
* the current ncurses source archive from invisible-island.net; and
* readline at pinned revision 553d6bb272f26400d6d4d1cac7c1df84c447449b
  from the sailfishos-mirror/readline GitHub repository.

fmt, ncurses and readline do not need to be installed beforehand.  CMake
handles the downloads and archive extraction, so separate curl, tar and unzip
programs are not required.

The dependency build needs a writable source tree.  It creates or updates
bin, lib, include/fmt, include/ncurses, include/readline, ncurses, readline and
share beneath the repository root.  The current ncurses configuration may
also install terminal descriptions in /usr/local/share/terminfo, which must be
writable for that installation step.

Complete native build and test
------------------------------

From the repository root:

    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

The default top-level native configuration enables examples, CREPL, tests and
benchmarks.  It produces build/crepl, build/combdsl_example,
build/combdsl_single_step, the test executables and
build/combdsl_find_benchmark.  CREPL is also copied to bin/crepl.

The principal options are:

    -DCOMBDSL_BUILD_EXAMPLES=ON|OFF
    -DCOMBDSL_BUILD_TESTS=ON|OFF
    -DCOMBDSL_BUILD_BENCHMARKS=ON|OFF
    -DCOMBDSL_BUILD_BROWSER=ON|OFF

Set an option to OFF to omit its targets and their associated tool checks.

Benchmarks
----------

Use a Release build for meaningful native benchmark timings:

    cmake -S . -B build-release -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
    cmake --build build-release --target combdsl_find_benchmark
    ./build-release/combdsl_find_benchmark

The exhaustive quad benchmark is not part of the default benchmark run and
can take minutes:

    ./build-release/combdsl_find_benchmark --case quad

Combinator Studio WebAssembly build
-----------------------------------

Activate Emscripten, use a separate build directory, and provide a writable
Emscripten cache:

    EM_CACHE="$PWD/build-emscripten-cache" emcmake cmake -S . -B build-browser \
        -DCOMBDSL_BUILD_BROWSER=ON \
        -DCOMBDSL_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF \
        -DCOMBDSL_BUILD_TESTS=OFF \
        -DCOMBDSL_BUILD_BENCHMARKS=OFF \
        -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
    EM_CACHE="$PWD/build-emscripten-cache" \
        cmake --build build-browser --target combdsl_browser_docs

The combdsl_browser_docs target refreshes the static application in docs,
including combdsl.js and combdsl.wasm.  Serve docs over HTTP; file URLs cannot
start the Web Worker and fetch the WebAssembly module.  For example, when
Python is installed:

    python3 -m http.server 8000 --directory docs

Then open http://localhost:8000/.

Sites publishing
----------------

Publishing is separate from compiling this repository.  It requires Node.js,
npm, configured OpenAI Sites tooling and credentials, and a separate Sites
packaging workspace.  A normal native or Emscripten build does not publish or
deploy anything.
